UPDATE public.economics_cases SET tactical_explanations = ARRAY['FALSE — This statement is false. Niche marketing to households with strong sustainability attitudes means selling one identical product to the entire national market.

The relevant theory comes from market segmentation and targeting. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

The statement overreaches because niche marketing narrows focus; one identical national product is mass marketing. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. A segment defined for a federal state capital and outlying districts is measurable when survey and sales data reveal its size and average purchasing power.

The scenario is a worked example of market segmentation and targeting. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

In this setting, data on a federal state capital and outlying districts can make the geographic segment measurable in size and purchasing power. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Segment marketing to parents with young children and empty-nest couples means ignoring subgroup differences and promoting one undifferentiated offer.

This statement draws on market segmentation and targeting. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

Applied to this claim, segment marketing tailors to parents with young children and empty-nest couples; ignoring differences describes mass marketing. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Positioning toward weekend-only recreational users is unnecessary if the firm already completed geographic segmentation.

The scenario is a worked example of market segmentation and targeting. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

Applied to this claim, positioning still creates identity for the chosen subgroup after segmentation and targeting. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — This statement is false. Targeting weekend-only recreational users means listing every possible subgroup without evaluating attractiveness.

The topic is market segmentation and targeting, but the decisive skill here is reading quantifiers. Words such as never, always, only, or all turn a generally true idea into a claim that can be rejected by one routine counterexample.

The statement overreaches because targeting evaluates attractiveness before selecting weekend-only recreational users or any subgroup. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Watch the absolute wording "every": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.'] WHERE case_id = 'CASE 5.6.51' AND tier = 'full';
UPDATE public.economics_cases SET tactical_explanations = ARRAY['FALSE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is false. Economies of scale for a niche offer to households with strong sustainability attitudes are identical to those for undifferentiated mass production.

The relevant theory comes from market segmentation and targeting. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

The statement overreaches because niche focus on households with strong sustainability attitudes typically lacks the very large identical output that drives scale economies. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.', 'FALSE — This statement is false. A firm serving parents with young children and empty-nest couples with strategic fit should spread resources evenly across every segment in the economy.

The topic is market segmentation and targeting, but the decisive skill here is reading quantifiers. Words such as never, always, only, or all turn a generally true idea into a claim that can be rejected by one routine counterexample.

The statement overreaches because strategic fit implies focus on chosen segments such as parents with young children and empty-nest couples, not equal coverage of all segments. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Watch the absolute wording "every": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is false. Producing a tiny customised batch for households with strong sustainability attitudes always yields lower unit cost than mass production of identical units.

The topic is market segmentation and targeting, but the decisive skill here is reading quantifiers. Words such as never, always, only, or all turn a generally true idea into a claim that can be rejected by one routine counterexample.

The statement overreaches because small customised batches for households with strong sustainability attitudes do not automatically enjoy mass-production scale economies. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Watch the absolute wording "always": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.', 'FALSE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is false. Grouping buyers by students and mid-career adults alone is geographic segmentation because demographics describe place.

Here you must apply ideas from market segmentation and targeting to a concrete situation rather than recite a definition from memory. Ownership, customer type, stakeholder group, or country of production usually decides the verdict.

The statement overreaches because students and mid-career adults are demographic, not geographic segmentation. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.', 'TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. A segment of first-time buyers and long-established residents is profitable only if expected revenue from serving them exceeds the cost of tailored offers.

Here you must apply ideas from market segmentation and targeting to a concrete situation rather than recite a definition from memory. Ownership, customer type, stakeholder group, or country of production usually decides the verdict.

Applied carefully, profitability for first-time buyers and long-established residents requires revenue to cover the cost of serving that demographic subgroup. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.'] WHERE case_id = 'CASE 5.6.52' AND tier = 'full';
UPDATE public.economics_cases SET tactical_explanations = ARRAY['TRUE — The claim is correct. A segment of enthusiasts motivated by specialist taste and craft quality is accessible when advertising and retail channels can reach those customers effectively.

The scenario is a worked example of market segmentation and targeting. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

In this setting, accessible channels must reach enthusiasts motivated by specialist taste and craft quality for viable targeting. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'FALSE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is false. Grouping buyers in a northern province and southern retail catchment is psychographic segmentation because location reflects personal values.

Here you must apply ideas from market segmentation and targeting to a concrete situation rather than recite a definition from memory. Ownership, customer type, stakeholder group, or country of production usually decides the verdict.

The statement overreaches because buyers in a northern province and southern retail catchment illustrate geographic, not psychographic segmentation. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. A segment of bulk-order institutional buyers versus single-unit shoppers is durable when usage patterns remain stable enough to justify product and promotion planning.

This tests discrimination within market segmentation and targeting: local versus international scope, equity versus debt, product versus market orientation, and similar pairs.

Applied carefully, durability for bulk-order institutional buyers versus single-unit shoppers means the behavioural pattern persists long enough for planning. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. Mass marketing of a universal staple to a federal state capital and outlying districts and other areas uses one offer promoted in almost the same way everywhere.

The relevant theory comes from market segmentation and targeting. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, a single staple promoted uniformly across a federal state capital and outlying districts and elsewhere fits mass marketing. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. Targeting price-conscious buyers accepting older technology is behavioural segmentation because attitudes are the same as purchase frequency.

The scenario is a worked example of market segmentation and targeting. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

Applied to this claim, price-conscious buyers accepting older technology reflect psychographic, not purely behavioural segmentation. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.'] WHERE case_id = 'CASE 5.6.53' AND tier = 'full';
UPDATE public.economics_cases SET tactical_explanations = ARRAY['TRUE — This statement is correct. Segment marketing to first-time buyers and long-established residents would offer differentiated products matching the subgroup''s identified needs rather than one identical item.

